window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"][24]={"university_biology_en:M24-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M24-L1 – Neural circuits<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Neuroscience and Behavioural Research<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L2<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L1<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M21-L3<\/a>: membrane potential,\nsynapses and dynamical models.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Model synaptic integration\nquantitatively, distinguish circuit motifs and explain the limits of\nlinear approximations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A neural circuit processes signals through interactions among many\ncells. The function of a connection depends not only on the transmitter\nbut also on receptors, reversal potentials, cellular state and temporal\ncontext. Anatomy identifies possible signal paths, but does not\nautomatically specify their functional strengths.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"integration-and-excitability\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Integration and excitability<\/h3>\n<p>Excitatory synaptic inputs typically increase the probability of an\naction potential, whereas inhibitory inputs reduce it. In a\nconductance-based description, an input contributes membrane current\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_i=g_i(E_i-V)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith inward current defined as positive here.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis conductance,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe reversal potential and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe current membrane potential. An inhibitory input can attenuate other\nvoltage changes by increasing conductance even when it causes little\nhyperpolarisation (shunting inhibition).<\/p>\n<p>For small deviations from the resting potential, temporal integration\ncan be approximated as<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta V(t)=\\sum_{t_i\\leq t} a_i\\exp[-(t-t_i)/\\tau].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Amplitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin mV is positive or negative, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a common time constant. This model ignores nonlinear dendrites,\nchanging conductances and reset after a spike. In a threshold model, a\nspike occurs once the potential reaches a boundary; the calculation\nstops at that point.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"circuit-motifs\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Circuit motifs<\/h3>\n<p>Converging inputs can integrate information from different sources.\nDivergence distributes a signal to different targets. Feedforward\ninhibition restricts the excitation time window; feedback inhibition\nresponds to output activity. Recurrent excitation may sustain activity;\nwithout stabilising mechanisms a linear model can become unstable. A\nfunctional circuit diagram therefore distinguishes direction, sign and\ntemporal dynamics. Correlated activity between two cells does not prove\na direct connection because both might receive a common input.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_125_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Excitatory and inhibitory inputs are integrated in a simplified threshold model.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Excitatory and inhibitory inputs are\nintegrated in a simplified threshold model.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Excitatory and inhibitory inputs are integrated in a\nsimplified threshold model.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>A fictional neuron rests at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>65<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-65<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmV and has a model threshold of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>55<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-55<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmV. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmV input arrives; 10 ms later another\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmV input and a simultaneous\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmV inhibitory input arrive. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nms, the sum at 10 ms is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7.64<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6e^{-0.5}+6-2=7.64<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmV. The potential is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>57.36<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-57.36<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmV, below threshold. Without inhibition it would be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>55.36<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-55.36<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmV, still just below threshold. Two simultaneous\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmV inputs without inhibition would cross the model threshold.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the remaining amplitude of an 8 mV input after 20 ms with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nms.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Two simultaneous inputs contribute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmV. What is the change in the linear model?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is an anatomical contact insufficient evidence of functional\nexcitation?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.94<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8e^{-1}=2.94<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmV.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmV.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Receptors, reversal potential, synaptic strength and current\ncellular state determine the effect; a contact alone does not.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>All numbers are simulated. Linear summation does not model\nvoltage-dependent channels, refractory periods or shunting inhibition.\nThe threshold is a teaching constant, not a universal physiological\nmeasurement.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Circuits are dynamical systems. Studying their function requires\nappropriate measurement methods.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M24-L2<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK11104/\">Purves et\nal. (2001) – Neuroscience, 2nd ed.: Summation of Synaptic Potentials<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· Synaptic integration; indexed section · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M24-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M24-L2 – Neurophysiological measurements<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Neuroscience and Behavioural Research<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L1<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L1<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L3<\/a>: neural signals,\nmicroscopy and measurement quality.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish electrical and\noptical readouts, calculate sampling intervals and fluorescence changes,\nand evaluate measurement artefacts.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Neurophysiological methods capture different aspects of neural\nactivity. An electrical signal is not equivalent to an optical indicator\nor a haemodynamic signal. Method choice depends on the biological\nquestion, spatial scale and required temporal resolution.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"electrical-and-optical-measurement-principles\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Electrical and\noptical measurement principles<\/h3>\n<p>Intracellular voltage recordings capture potential changes in\nindividual cells. A voltage clamp regulates a target potential and uses\nthe required current as a readout. Extracellular recordings capture\nelectrical fields; assigning individual spike shapes to cells requires\nadditional model assumptions and quality checks. Local field potentials\nreflect summed, spatially weighted currents and are not directly the\nmean firing rate of all nearby neurons.<\/p>\n<p>Calcium indicators provide an optical readout related to\nintracellular calcium. A common relative representation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta F/F_0=(F-F_0)/F_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe baseline\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust be justified and positive. Sensor kinetics, saturation, motion and\nphotobleaching influence the signal. A fluorescence peak therefore does\nnot automatically represent one spike. Functional magnetic resonance\nimaging primarily measures haemodynamic changes, not direct action\npotentials.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"temporal-resolution-and-sampling\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Temporal resolution and\nsampling<\/h3>\n<p>With regular sampling at frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe interval is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/f_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor ideal band-limited continuous signals, the Nyquist boundary is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\ncomponents above it can appear as spurious lower frequencies. An\nappropriate anti-aliasing filter acts before digitisation. For short\npulses, merely specifying a formal Nyquist limit is insufficient: sensor\nbandwidth, signal-to-noise ratio and enough samples determine the\nrecoverable shape. Comparative studies synchronise time bases and\npreserve raw data and processing parameters.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_126_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two brief electrical events and a slower schematic optical response share a time axis.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two brief electrical events and a slower\nschematic optical response share a time axis.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Two brief electrical events and a slower schematic\noptical response share a time axis.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>A simulated electrical recording has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10000<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s=10000<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nHz. The sampling interval is 0.1 ms and the Nyquist boundary 5000 Hz. A\n2 ms event spans approximately 20 sampling intervals. An optical\nrecording at 20 frames/s instead has a 50 ms frame interval. With a\nbaseline of 200 and peak of 250 intensity units,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta F/F_0=0.25=25\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA slower optical response can combine several fast electrical events.\nAmplitudes from the two methods cannot be compared directly without\ncalibration.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the interval and Nyquist boundary at 2000 Hz.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta F/F_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>150<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_0=150<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>180<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=180<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Give two reasons why a fluorescence trace and an electrical spike\ntrain need not coincide in time.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">0.5 ms; 1000 Hz.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>180<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>150<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>150<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(180-150)/150=0.20=20\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For example, slow sensor kinetics and low frame rate. Motion and\nnonlinear calcium dynamics may also complicate matching.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The examples are simulations, not experimental protocols. Sampling\ntheory assumes idealised band limitation. Optical signal amplitude and\nspike count need not be linearly related.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>A measurement’s meaning depends on its signal model. The next lesson\nexamines statistical analysis of sensory decisions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M24-L3<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6667546/\">Approaches and\nLimitations in the Investigation of Synaptic Transmission and Plasticity\n(2019)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Electrophysiology and optical readouts; indexed article\ntext · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M24-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M24-L3 – Sensory systems and behaviour<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Neuroscience and Behavioural Research<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L2<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L7<\/a>: normal distribution\nand sensory processing.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish hits and false\nalarms, calculate sensory sensitivity and response criterion, and\ninterpret behavioural data critically.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Sensory systems transform physical stimuli into neural activity.\nDecisions are not determined by stimulus strength alone: internal\nvariability, attention, expectations and the costs of different errors\ncan influence responses. Psychophysics studies relationships between\ncontrolled stimuli and observed decisions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"signal-detection-theory\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Signal detection theory<\/h3>\n<p>A yes–no task distinguishes trials with and without a signal. Four\noutcomes are possible: hit, miss, false alarm and correct rejection. Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe the hit proportion among signal trials and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe false alarm proportion among no-signal trials. The equal-variance\nGaussian model separates sensitivity and decision criterion:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d&#39;=\\Phi^{-1}(H)-\\Phi^{-1}(F),\\qquad c=-\\tfrac12[\\Phi^{-1}(H)+\\Phi^{-1}(F)].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Phi^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the standard normal quantile function. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe criterion is conservative relative to the midpoint of the\ndistributions; for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit is liberal. In the model,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d&#39;=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindicates equal distribution means. Proportions of exactly zero or one\nproduce infinite quantiles; finite samples require prespecified\ncorrections or directly fitted models. An ROC curve shows hit rate\nagainst false alarm rate at different criteria. It permits comparison\nbeyond a single response threshold.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"behaviour-and-biological-interpretation\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Behaviour and\nbiological interpretation<\/h3>\n<p>Neural tuning curves describe responses to different stimuli. A\ndecoder can estimate stimulus class from an activity pattern; success on\nindependent test data demonstrates available information, not\nnecessarily its actual use by the animal. Behavioural studies must\ndistinguish motivation, motor performance and sensory limitations.\nRepeated trials from the same person are not independent biological\nreplicates. Estimating group differences consequently requires\nhierarchical models or person-level summaries.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_127_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two overlapping equal-variance normal distributions and a decision criterion between their means.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two overlapping equal-variance normal\ndistributions and a decision criterion between their means.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Two overlapping equal-variance normal distributions and a\ndecision criterion between their means.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>In a fictional task, 100 signal trials yield 80 hits and 100\nno-signal trials yield 20 false alarms. Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.842<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z(H)=0.842<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.842<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z(F)=-0.842<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.683<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d&#39;\\approx1.683<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt another criterion let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.645<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d&#39;\\approx1.645<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.822<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c\\approx0.822<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nObserved hit rates differ substantially, while estimated sensitivity is\nsimilar. Statistical inference would be required to establish a\nsensitivity difference.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the hit and false alarm rates for 90 hits out of 100 signal\ntrials and 10 false alarms out of 100 no-signal trials.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>d<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d&#39;<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nusing quantiles\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>±<\/mi><mn>1.282<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pm1.282<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can a higher hit rate occur with unchanged sensitivity?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=0.9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.564<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d&#39;\\approx2.564<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A more liberal response criterion can increase both hits and false\nalarms.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The data are simulated. The formulas assume normally distributed\ninternal evidence with equal variance. A single\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>d<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d&#39;<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoes not fully describe sensory or cognitive performance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Signal detection separates response bias from sensitivity. The next\nlesson examines how expectations and neural connections change through\nexperience.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M24-L4<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://wise.cgu.edu/wise-tutorials/tutorial-signal-detection-theory/signal-detection-d-defined-2/\">Claremont\nGraduate University – WISE: Signal Detection, d′ Defined<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nSensitivity and normal quantiles; tutorial · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M24-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M24-L4 – Learning and plasticity<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Neuroscience and Behavioural Research<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L6<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L2<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M24-L1<\/a>: behaviour,\nsignalling and neural integration.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish synaptic plasticity\nacross timescales, calculate a prediction-error model and separate\nbiological from algorithmic explanations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Learning refers to experience-dependent changes in behaviour or\nprocessing. Plasticity encompasses changes in neural properties,\nsynaptic strength and network organisation. Not every short-term\nbehavioural change is learning; fatigue, sensory adaptation or motor\nlimitations may produce similar observations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"synaptic-and-systemic-plasticity\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Synaptic and systemic\nplasticity<\/h3>\n<p>Short-term plasticity changes transmission strength over brief\nperiods, for example through changes in synaptic vesicle availability or\npresynaptic calcium dynamics. Long-term potentiation (LTP) and long-term\ndepression (LTD) denote persistent strengthening and weakening of\nsynaptic transmission respectively. At certain hippocampal synapses,\nNMDA receptors and calcium signalling contribute to induction; other\nsynapses use other mechanisms. Long-term expression can involve changes\nin receptor distribution and gene expression. Homeostatic plasticity\nstabilises network activity, whereas associative plasticity can alter\nspecific connections. Memory therefore cannot be equated with a single\nsynaptic mechanism.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"learning-as-prediction-error\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Learning as prediction error<\/h3>\n<p>A simple associative model for one cue describes the expected outcome\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non a normalised scale. Let the observed outcome be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the learning rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;\\alpha\\leq1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{t+1}=V_t+\\alpha(\\lambda_t-V_t).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The term in parentheses is the prediction error. For constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_t=1-(1-\\alpha)^t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhen the outcome is no longer presented, the model prediction falls by a\nfactor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper trial. This single-cue model is a reduced form of error-based\nassociative models. It captures neither all attentional processes nor\ncontext-dependent return of learned responses. The parameter is not a\ndirectly measured biological synaptic rate.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_128_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Expectation rises with repeated outcomes and falls after omission; learning rate 0.25.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Expectation rises with repeated outcomes\nand falls after omission; learning rate 0.25.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Expectation rises with repeated outcomes and falls after\nomission; learning rate 0.25.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>For a fictional cue let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_1=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4375<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_2=0.4375<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.578125<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_3=0.578125<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the expected outcome is omitted on the fourth trial\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_3=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>),\nthe prediction falls to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.433594<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_4=0.433594<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(rounded). Separately, a simulated synaptic recording shows a mean\nresponse rising from 2 to 3 mV: a 50% increase. These figures describe\ndifferent levels; synaptic amplitude cannot be derived from the model\nlearning rate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the next value when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsubsequently.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does persistent synaptic strengthening alone not demonstrate a\nparticular memory?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_1=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_2=0.75<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.375<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_3=0.75+0.5(0-0.75)=0.375<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Memory requires a link to behaviour and network function; synaptic\nstrengthening can have multiple causes and is not specific to memory\ncontent.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Data are simulated. The model assumes a fixed learning rate, one cue\nand a scalar outcome. Extinction in the model is a declining prediction,\nnot proof of biological memory erasure.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Plasticity connects experience with altered processing. Robust\nevidence requires an ethically and methodologically appropriate research\ndesign.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M24-L5<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK11136/\">Purves et\nal. (2001) – Mechanism of Long-Term Synaptic Plasticity in the Mammalian\nNervous System<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · LTP and LTD; indexed section · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4862921/\">Mini-review:\nPrediction errors, attention and associative learning (2016)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nAssociative learning and prediction error; indexed article text · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M24-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M24-L5 – Research ethics and model limitations<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M24 – Neuroscience and Behavioural Research<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L2<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M19-L3<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M24-L2<\/a>: research ethics,\nexperimental design and measurement models.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Identify experimental units,\nrecognise pseudoreplication quantitatively and justify ethical and\nmethodological limits of neuroscience models.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Neurobiological research connects molecular, cellular, organismal and\nhuman observational levels. Ethical acceptability and scientific\nvalidity are distinct but closely related prerequisites. Unreliable\nstudies can cause burdens without producing commensurate knowledge.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"ethics-and-evidence\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Ethics and evidence<\/h3>\n<p>The 3Rs require consideration of alternative methods (Replacement),\navoidance of unnecessary animal numbers (Reduction) and minimisation of\nburdens (Refinement). Reducing sample size must not result in an\nunusable design. ARRIVE 2.0 supports transparent reporting of animal\nresearch, especially design, sample size, exclusions, randomisation,\nblinding, outcomes and statistical methods. A reporting standard does\nnot replace regulatory or institutional approval. For human research,\ninformed consent, confidentiality, voluntary participation and\nappropriate review are essential. Identifiable behavioural and neural\ndata require particular protection.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"experimental-unit-and-model-limitations\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Experimental unit and\nmodel limitations<\/h3>\n<p>The experimental unit is the smallest unit independently assigned to\na condition. If assignment occurs at animal level, additional measured\ncells per animal increase measurement information, not the number of\nindependent animals. For equal clusters of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeasurements and intraclass correlation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ρ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe design effect\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=1+(m-1)\\rho<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nillustrates the impact of clustering. For a simple mean with equal\ncluster sizes and equal variance, approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_\\mathrm{eff}=n/D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis does not calculate the degrees of freedom of a treatment effect.\nHierarchical models or analysis at experimental-unit level account for\nactual assignment.<\/p>\n<p>Transferability depends on which biological properties a model\nshares. Cell cultures and organoids are useful for certain mechanisms\nbut do not reproduce complete behaviour. Animal-model findings cannot be\ntransferred to human experiences or clinical efficacy without further\nevidence. Correlation, causal mechanism and external validity require\nseparate assessment.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_129_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two groups with six independent animals each and nested measurements.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two groups with six independent animals\neach and nested measurements.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Two groups with six independent animals each and nested\nmeasurements.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>In a wholly simulated dataset, two groups of six animals each are\ncompared, with ten measurements per animal. There are 120 individual\nmeasurements and twelve experimental units if assignment is independent\nat animal level. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=0.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=1+9\\cdot0.4=4.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>26.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_\\mathrm{eff}\\approx26.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes only the precision of the hypothetical overall mean relative\nto independent individual measurements. The twelve animals remain the\nindependent units for group comparison. A naive test treating 120 values\nas independent would be inappropriate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How many independent units are present with eight animals and five\ncell measurements per animal when assignment is per animal?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does a complete ARRIVE checklist replace ethical approval?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Eight animals; 40 measurements.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=1+4\\cdot0.25=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nfor a simple mean this corresponds to approximately 20 independent\nindividual measurements.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. Transparent reporting and legally or institutionally required\napprovals serve different purposes.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The examples contain no real experimental data and provide no\nintervention instructions. The design-effect approximation assumes equal\ncluster sizes and common correlation. Complex designs need separate\nplanning and appropriate statistical models.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>This elective integrates circuits, measurement methods, behaviour,\nlearning and responsible research. Subsequent master’s profiles consider\nother levels of biological research.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M25-L1<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7360023/\">Percie\ndu Sert et al. (2020) – ARRIVE guidelines 2.0<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Essential 10,\nexperimental unit and bias; indexed article text · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://nc3rs.org.uk/who-we-are/3rs\">NC3Rs – The 3Rs<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nDefinitions of the 3Rs · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
